🚀 PROBLEM: MINIMUM NUMBER OF ARROWS TO BURST BALLOONS
🔹 Problem Statement

You are given a 2D array points where
points[i] = [start, end] represents the horizontal diameter of a balloon.

An arrow can be shot vertically from any x-coordinate and bursts all balloons whose range includes that x-coordinate.

👉 Return the minimum number of arrows required to burst all balloons.

🔹 Why this problem is IMPORTANT

✔ Classic Greedy Interval problem
✔ Appears simple but tests thinking clarity
✔ Very common in Amazon / Google / Microsoft
✔ Similar idea reused in meeting rooms, interval scheduling

🔹 Key Idea 💡 (Greedy Strategy)

Shoot arrows at the end of intervals.

Why?

Shooting at the end gives the maximum chance to burst future balloons.

🔹 Algorithm (Step by Step)

Sort balloons by end coordinate

Shoot first arrow at end of first balloon

For each next balloon:

If its start > arrowPosition

Need a new arrow

Update arrow position to current balloon’s end

Count arrows

🔹 Dry Run Example 🧠
Input:
points = [[10,16],[2,8],[1,6],[7,12]]

Step 1 – Sort by end:
[[1,6], [2,8], [7,12], [10,16]]

Step 2 – Process:

Arrow at 6 → bursts [1,6], [2,8]

Next start = 7 > 6 → need new arrow

Arrow at 12 → bursts [7,12], [10,16]

✅ Answer = 2

🔹 Java Code (Clean & Interview Ready)
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int findMinArrowShots(int[][] points) {

        if (points == null || points.length == 0)
            return 0;

        // Sort based on ending position
        Arrays.sort(points, (a, b) -> Integer.compare(a[1], b[1]));

        int arrows = 1;
        int arrowPos = points[0][1];

        for (int i = 1; i < points.length; i++) {
            // If current balloon starts after arrow position
            if (points[i][0] > arrowPos) {
                arrows++;
                arrowPos = points[i][1];
            }
        }
        return arrows;
    }
}

